  "articleBody": "Interior woodwork defines the quality of a renovation, but overlooked installation details often result in loose joints and warped trim. Avoid these common carpentry missteps.\n\nInstalling before wet trades finish: Placing millwork while drywall mud, texture, or tile mortar is actively drying causes wood to absorb moisture and swell, opening joints later.\n\nMeasuring rough openings inaccurately: Forgetting to verify plumb, level, and floor clearances leads to sticking interior doors and uneven margins.\n\nRelying on caulking to hide wide gaps: Caulk shrinks over time. Proper carpentry relies on tight miter and cope joints rather than heavy beads of sealant.\n\nIgnoring wall and floor irregularities: Nailing baseboards flat against wavy drywall without shimming or scribing produces bowed baseboards and loose miters.\n\nUsing weak fasteners or missing wall studs: Shooting finish nails purely into sheetrock leaves moldings loose. Fasteners must bite firmly into solid wall framing.\n\nFailing to acclimate timber: Installing dry trim straight off the delivery truck into a conditioned home without acclimation leads to shrinkage and cracked joints.\n\nWhat to Do Next: HH Remodeling LLC delivers detail-driven finish carpentry, custom framing, and trim installation across Denver, Centennial, and Littleton. Reach out to owner Abraham Becerra at 303-505-0866 for a dependable estimate on your trim project.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
